00:04:38.667 --> 00:04:43.933
So, yeah, it was a
very, very recent thing.

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:49.200
Steve: It's interesting.
It's interesting.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:52.200
Of course, I think we both agree,
at least certainly, in my view,

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:57.100
reading is such a powerful
way to improve in a language

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:59.633
and an important goal
in learning a language.

00:04:59.633 --> 00:05:01.267
When you're dealing with
the different writing system

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:03.467
then you have you
know, it's a major obstacle.

00:05:03.467 --> 00:05:05.800
And we never quite
get as comfortable

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:08.500
in a different writing
system as we are in our own.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:10.733
So as a Spanish speaker,
you're learning English

00:05:10.733 --> 00:05:12.133
or learning German,
you're learning Italian,

00:05:12.133 --> 00:05:13.867
it's the same writing system.

00:05:13.867 --> 00:05:16.167
So it's a lot easier
from that point of view

00:05:16.167 --> 00:05:17.633
to get right into it.

00:05:17.633 --> 00:05:23.867
But I think regardless of the
obstacles and the difficulties,

00:05:23.867 --> 00:05:26.833
I still believe we have
to enjoy the language

00:05:26.833 --> 00:05:28.267
or we aren't going to succeed.

00:05:28.267 --> 00:05:31.833
Maybe we can now speak
a little bit of spanish.

00:05:31.833 --> 00:05:33.233
Alberto: Yes, of course.

00:05:33.233 --> 00:05:38.400
Steve: You have to do it with pleasure

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.567
and enjoy the language learning process.

00:05:40.567 --> 00:05:46.900
Cause if we focus on grammar, 

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:51.833
and the basic rules of language
as an obligation,

00:05:51.833 --> 00:06:00.733
because we have exams,
making linguistic growth is very challenging.
123
00:06:00,733 --&gt; 00:06:02,033
What do you think?

00:06:02.033 --> 00:06:09.100
Alberto: I think it's frustrating 
for many people more than just difficult.
125
00:06:10,533 --&gt; 00:06:13,167
Because about a language, 

00:06:13.167 --> 00:06:15.000
and this is what I always tell people,

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.767
Learning a language is not difficult; 
rather, it is challenging.

00:06:19.767 --> 00:06:23.600
And I'm referring to complicated 
because it's not like that

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:27.067
I'll be learning English in classes for six months.

00:06:27.067 --> 00:06:30.300
No. It heavily incorporates 
the teacher's methodology

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:32.000
or the materials they are using.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:34.967
Or, as you say, Steve, 
how much you like it.

00:06:34.967 --> 00:06:39.133
Because it's very easy to give up something 
you don't like after a month.

00:06:39.133 --> 00:06:43.333
And I frequently use the same analogy 
when referring to the gym.

00:06:43.333 --> 00:06:45.833
To me, learning a language and exercising

00:06:45.833 --> 00:06:47.433
are almost identical.

00:06:47.433 --> 00:06:49.300
It requires having a lot of willpower

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:51.800
to do it continuously day after day.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:55.633
In my opinion, doing it yourself

00:06:55.633 --> 00:06:57.767
through self-learning works best.

00:06:57.767 --> 00:06:59.900
at your own pace, 
doing the things you enjoy.

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:02.533
That is the problem with the school,

00:07:02.533 --> 00:07:07.033
they believe the content you are 
consuming right now is better.

00:07:07.033 --> 00:07:13.133
The audios and role plays you do, 

00:07:13.133 --> 00:07:15.633
are those that the school consider the best.

00:07:15.633 --> 00:07:20.300
And frequently it's not the best environment 
for your specific learning.

00:07:21.533 --> 00:07:24.967
I understand that it must be universal,  

00:07:24.967 --> 00:07:27.767
in order to function for everyone as a whole.

00:07:27.767 --> 00:07:30.633
And that´s why I think that is the major flaw

00:07:30.633 --> 00:07:33.533
in the instruction at the schools.

00:07:33.533 --> 00:07:36.267
An Instructor that lead you by the hand. 

00:07:36.267 --> 00:07:40.200
It's possible that you aren't interested
 in what you're learning.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:43.133
And as you say, that is the most essential thing.

00:07:43.133 --> 00:07:44.400
to learn a language.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.167
That you are interested in it and enjoy it, 
as you correctly stated.

00:07:46.167 --> 00:07:51.567
Steve: There is an order, 
according to Steven Krashen's theory.

00:07:51.567 --> 00:07:54.833
In learning language structures, words,

00:07:54.833 --> 00:07:56.633
or other linguistic facets.

00:07:56.634 --> 00:07:58.201
There is no inherent order.

00:07:59.467 --> 00:08:07.233
Whatever the professor may teach 
about the subjunctive and other concepts,

00:08:07.233 --> 00:08:08.800
the English "tenses."

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:12.033
But you will learn those things according to this order.

00:08:12.033 --> 00:08:14.300
No need to worry.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:17.967
And as a consequence, exams and texts

00:08:17.967 --> 00:08:26.267
make you anxious about test results in particular.

00:08:26.267 --> 00:08:32.933
And refrain from letting any language 
enter your head.

00:08:32.933 --> 00:08:42.867
Therefore, you must believe
in this innate learning process.

00:08:42.867 --> 00:08:47.467
I believe that the way that 
language is taught in schools,

00:08:47.467 --> 00:08:51.700
destroys this sense of independence.

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:56.233
Everything is according to the teacher's words.

00:08:56.233 --> 00:08:58.800
"You're going to read this content."

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:02.367
" Now that I have taught you this,

00:09:02.367 --> 00:09:08.500
let's see if you have understood
 what I have taught you."

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:12.567
But ultimately, 
the head cannot function in this way.

00:09:12.567 --> 00:09:17.867
The way that is taught in schools 
is therefore against nature.

00:09:17.867 --> 00:09:23.967
And above all, because the learner 
lacks this independence,

00:09:23.967 --> 00:09:26.900
which is crucial in language learning.

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:30.233
Alberto: Yes, and I believe that many people 
believe this when they hear, for instance,

00:09:30.233 --> 00:09:33.567
that I learned Alemán by watching Netflix.

00:09:33.567 --> 00:09:38.133
And then I did a little shadowing practice.

00:09:38.133 --> 00:09:41.167
What I learned the most from was playing 
computer games and watching Netflix.

00:09:41.167 --> 00:09:46.300
Many people take that to be true, especially in Mexico,

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:48.233
because this is my own unique experience.

00:09:48.233 --> 00:09:52.233
People frequently say, 
"You're lying to me,"

00:09:52.233 --> 00:09:55.300
You're trying to tell me this..."

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:57.967
Unquestionably, there is a strong opposition to

00:09:57.967 --> 00:10:01.167
the notion of learning naturally, as you claim.

00:10:01.167 --> 00:10:07.600
And I think my hypothesis for why this continues 
to be the case today is that,

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:11.633
despite all the content that is already available,

00:10:11.633 --> 00:10:16.300
90% of people find it to be ineffective,

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:17.333
For stating a number, right?

00:10:17.333 --> 00:10:20.833
Because I believe it works better 
for those who are more logical.

00:10:20.833 --> 00:10:24.033
Like when they say, "A, B, C, D, perfect."

00:10:24.033 --> 00:10:25.767
And they are building it as well.

00:10:25.767 --> 00:10:27.067
But they are a minority.

00:10:27.067 --> 00:10:29.400
I believe that in order to learn in this way

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:31.133
you must be a genius.

00:10:31.133 --> 00:10:33.333
That is my belief.
It's my opinion.

00:10:33.333 --> 00:10:37.467
I believe that the vast majority of us learn naturally.

00:10:37.467 --> 00:10:38.500
Simply filling us to the brim.

00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:39.300
And that is what I tell the students,

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:40.833
"Speak to the language, wherever it may be. 

00:10:40.833 --> 00:10:41.500
No matter"

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:45.867
No initial step exists.

00:10:45.867 --> 00:10:47.767
Because that "I know basic English" stuff…

00:10:47.767 --> 00:10:51.433
"I'll start with the basics and work my way up," 
seems unusual to me.

00:10:51.433 --> 00:10:55.833
And I think that stems from the notion of how

00:10:55.833 --> 00:10:56.867
text-based library structures.

00:10:56.867 --> 00:10:59.567
They start off by using the simple present.

00:10:59.567 --> 00:11:01.400
Then, the present continuous comes after
the simple present.

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.567
After the present continuous, simple past, and so on.

00:11:03.567 --> 00:11:07.700
This leads to the delusion 
that the present perfect

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:14.333
is an advanced topic while
the simple present is a basic topic..

00:11:14.333 --> 00:11:16.500
When that isn't true.

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:20.133
Simply put, there are various contexts
in which the language is used.

00:11:20.133 --> 00:11:21.500
But I think that's the reason.

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:24.333
For a teacher, I think it's easier 
to prepare a lesson plan

00:11:24.333 --> 00:11:25.633
and set up a schedule.

00:11:25.633 --> 00:11:28.367
I'm talking about a school curriculum.

00:11:28.367 --> 00:11:34.633
If it is based on skills like pieces 
or linguistic chunks,

00:11:34.633 --> 00:11:40.333
a class of understandable information, 
or a class of habits.

00:11:40.333 --> 00:11:42.367
That is very ambiguous.

00:11:42.367 --> 00:11:43.433
I believe it to be difficult.

00:11:43.433 --> 00:11:46.000
In fact, I'd like to know, Steve,
what you think about

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:52.133
how this contribution could be made in a classroom.

00:11:53.667 --> 00:11:57.433
Steve: Yes, this is very difficult, 
but we must acknowledge

00:11:57.433 --> 00:11:59.533
that the world has changed.

00:11:59.533 --> 00:12:05.767
And as you say, there are plenty 
of online contents now, like Netflix.

00:12:05.767 --> 00:12:13.633
And other things, so we don't need 
the teacher to explain the entire lesson to us..

00:12:13.633 --> 00:12:16.767
We must learn, byy using this content 
as a starting point, 

00:12:16.767 --> 00:12:19.133
Content is available everywhere.

00:12:19.133 --> 00:12:23.267
We can choose and look for interesting things.

00:12:23.267 --> 00:12:30.467
And perhaps students can study
 different subjects in one class.

00:12:30.467 --> 00:12:33.633
Three alumni: alumni A, B, and C.

00:12:33.633 --> 00:12:36.400
They will be studying through various contents.

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:42.467
Then they can discuss about 
the material they studied..

00:12:42.467 --> 00:12:47.167
But there is also the matter of the book

00:12:47.167 --> 00:12:51.767
I mentioned in the film, "Atomic Habits."

00:12:51.767 --> 00:12:54.200
Hábitos atómicos.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:13:04.533
Habits and activities must be developed 
on one's own.

00:13:04.533 --> 00:13:10.567
It's crucial that you engage in 
enjoyable learning activities

00:13:13.133 --> 00:13:20.033
every day or nearly every day.

00:13:20.033 --> 00:13:26.633
And that you have habits for doing things, 
listening to or reading, or speaking.

00:13:26.633 --> 00:13:28.500
Every day, every day, every day.

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:32.267
This is more significant than any exam.

00:13:32.267 --> 00:13:39.633
Because of this, we at LingQ 
measure activity levels

00:13:39.633 --> 00:13:44.767
rather than level, which is difficult.

00:13:44.767 --> 00:13:49.300
And if someone has good habits

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:57.200
and is involved with the language every day,
they will advance.

00:13:57.200 --> 00:14:03.633
Without making reference to any book or test.

00:14:05.167 --> 00:14:09.867
Alberto: Well, LingQ, it seems to be 
more based on reading, right?
252
00:14:09,867 --&gt; 00:14:12,600
Steve: No, no, because everything is...

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:15.733
I started out by listening.

00:14:15.733 --> 00:14:23.000
And while listening, 20% to 30% 
of what I hear is unclear.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:28.933
And after that, I'm going to read 
and save phrases and words.

00:14:28.933 --> 00:14:31.033
to study those words and phrases.

00:14:31.033 --> 00:14:34.233
There are many functions that help me understand

00:14:34.233 --> 00:14:35.333
words and phrases.

00:14:35.333 --> 00:14:44.033
But I always start with listening.

00:14:44.033 --> 00:14:48.300
I spend more time hearing than reading.

00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:51.767
But it's all part of the input, so.

00:14:52.833 --> 00:14:55.433
